1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,872
Saturday afternoon.

2
00:00:01,922 --> 00:00:07,464
Aziz is at his parents' house, and his mother wants a new washing machine, paid in parts.

3
00:00:07,514 --> 00:00:10,536
He opens the marketplace website on her old laptop.

4
00:00:10,586 --> 00:00:12,432
This browser has never seen him.

5
00:00:12,482 --> 00:00:15,912
It cannot check his face, and it cannot check his SIM card.

6
00:00:15,962 --> 00:00:21,024
So how does a website that knows nothing about this laptop trust that it is really Aziz?

7
00:00:21,074 --> 00:00:28,680
In Lesson 24, Aziz logged in on a new phone in the mobile bank: the TAF code, then an approval on his trusted phone.

8
00:00:28,730 --> 00:00:34,344
This lesson takes the same idea to the side apps, where there is no TAF and no MyID.

9
00:00:34,394 --> 00:00:36,552
First, what a side app cannot do.

10
00:00:36,602 --> 00:00:42,624
A browser cannot run the TAF part, so there is no verdict and no face confirmation.

11
00:00:42,674 --> 00:00:47,280
A side app never opens MyID, and it does not detect remote access.

12
00:00:47,330 --> 00:00:50,688
All of that lives only in the master app, the mobile bank.

13
00:00:50,738 --> 00:00:55,104
So a side app has the password and the bank's own SMS service.

14
00:00:55,154 --> 00:00:59,568
And it has one more tool, the best one: the master app itself.

15
00:00:59,618 --> 00:01:01,632
Start with a customer who is new.

16
00:01:01,682 --> 00:01:04,824
On the website, a new customer types the phone number.

17
00:01:04,874 --> 00:01:08,232
The bank's SMS service sends a one-time code.

18
00:01:08,282 --> 00:01:12,048
The customer types the code, then a password, two times.

19
00:01:12,098 --> 00:01:19,080
The platform makes a customer at level one, a device record for the browser, and a marketplace app account with one session.

20
00:01:19,130 --> 00:01:21,600
The browser is never a trusted device.

21
00:01:21,650 --> 00:01:24,552
And the new app account is marked not confirmed.

22
00:01:24,602 --> 00:01:28,704
It stays so until a TAF check in the mobile bank.

23
00:01:28,754 --> 00:01:31,680
Hold on to that mark: the next lesson is about it.

24
00:01:31,730 --> 00:01:33,552
Aziz is not new.

25
00:01:33,602 --> 00:01:35,880
He types his number and his password.

26
00:01:35,930 --> 00:01:38,208
The password always comes first.

27
00:01:38,258 --> 00:01:43,296
No code goes out, and no request goes anywhere, before the password is correct.

28
00:01:43,346 --> 00:01:47,064
Now the platform looks for a session of Aziz in the master app.

29
00:01:47,114 --> 00:01:48,936
He has one, on his phone.

30
00:01:48,986 --> 00:01:51,120
So the platform sends no code at all.

31
00:01:51,170 --> 00:01:56,016
It makes an approval request instead, and sends a push signal to the mobile bank.

32
00:01:56,066 --> 00:01:59,208
The push carries only the request identifier.

33
00:01:59,258 --> 00:02:03,936
The mobile bank pulls the request over its own session, and shows it to Aziz.

34
00:02:03,986 --> 00:02:10,992
It shows the marketplace, the browser, the network address, the time, and a warning never to give a code to anyone.

35
00:02:11,042 --> 00:02:14,232
Two buttons: Approve, and This was not me.

36
00:02:14,282 --> 00:02:16,104
Aziz taps Approve.

37
00:02:16,154 --> 00:02:20,712
The laptop session starts, and it gets the level of the session that approved it.

38
00:02:20,762 --> 00:02:26,496
Aziz passed MyID long ago, on this trusted phone, so the laptop session is at level two.

39
00:02:26,546 --> 00:02:33,600
That is the only way a side-app session reaches level two: an approval in the master app, from a customer at level two.

40
00:02:33,650 --> 00:02:36,432
I think this is the strongest part of the design.

41
00:02:36,482 --> 00:02:40,920
The approval travels over a session that the bank already trusts.

42
00:02:40,970 --> 00:02:46,152
And it carries no code, so nobody can phone Aziz and ask him to read one out.

43
00:02:46,202 --> 00:02:48,024
Now the other paths.

44
00:02:48,074 --> 00:02:50,664
Suppose Aziz misses the request.

45
00:02:50,714 --> 00:02:52,608
It waits for the configured time.

46
00:02:52,658 --> 00:02:57,240
Then the bank's SMS service sends a one-time code to his number.

47
00:02:57,290 --> 00:03:01,152
The code alone is enough, but the session is only at level one.

48
00:03:01,202 --> 00:03:07,320
A customer with no session in the master app gets the same: a code by SMS, and level one.

49
00:03:07,370 --> 00:03:09,432
And if Aziz taps This was not me?

50
00:03:09,482 --> 00:03:12,480
Someone knows his password, so his number goes on hold.

51
00:03:12,530 --> 00:03:14,928
Lesson 31 follows that path.

52
00:03:14,978 --> 00:03:18,000
The marketplace app on a new phone works the same way.

53
00:03:18,050 --> 00:03:25,272
When Aziz has a master-app session that passed the TAF check, the approval replaces the SMS code.

54
00:03:25,322 --> 00:03:31,344
Without one, the bank's SMS service sends a code, and the session starts at level one.

55
00:03:31,394 --> 00:03:35,592
Note what a side app never does at login: it never asks for MyID.

56
00:03:35,642 --> 00:03:42,072
Even for a customer who was at level two, a new phone or a long pause gives a side-app session at level one.

57
00:03:42,122 --> 00:03:44,472
Level two comes only after the approval.

58
00:03:44,522 --> 00:03:52,680
After each login, Aziz gets a notification by push and by SMS: a new web login, with the time and the browser.

59
00:03:52,730 --> 00:03:54,552
Back to the washing machine.

60
00:03:54,602 --> 00:04:00,240
Paying in parts needs level two, and the credit service reads the level of this session.

61
00:04:00,290 --> 00:04:04,320
With a session at level one, the marketplace would not open MyID.

62
00:04:04,370 --> 00:04:08,976
It would send Aziz to the mobile bank, because MyID runs only there.

63
00:04:09,026 --> 00:04:12,408
To recap: a side app asks for the password first.

64
00:04:12,458 --> 00:04:18,288
Then an approval in the master app replaces the code, or the bank's SMS service sends one.

65
00:04:18,338 --> 00:04:20,544
Only the approval can give level two.

66
00:04:20,594 --> 00:04:23,736
But remember the mark on a web account: not confirmed.

67
00:04:23,786 --> 00:04:28,632
Many customers make a marketplace account on the web before they ever install the mobile bank.

68
00:04:28,682 --> 00:04:33,696
When the same number arrives in the mobile bank, was that web account really made by this person?

69
00:04:33,746 --> 00:04:35,544
Next: the account link.
